  },
  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when your new faucet doesn't quite line up with your sink! This usually boils down to a few common issues. First, the number of mounting holes on your sink needs to match your faucet exactly. If your sink has three holes and your faucet only needs one, you'll have extra, unused holes unless you get a deck plate. Second, the spacing of those holes is critical. A widespread faucet, for example, needs more space between the handles than a single-hole or centerset one. Another big one is the faucet's reach and height. If your faucet is too short or too low, it might hit the backsplash or the window ledge behind your sink, making it hard to use or even impossible to install correctly. Also, consider the size of your sink bowl; a faucet that's too small might not reach far enough, leading to splashing, while one that's too big could overpower the sink. Finally, sometimes it's about the under-cabinet space and making sure the drain and supply lines can actually connect. If you're running into these kinds of snags, especially with unique sink or faucet designs, a professional like HH Remodeling LLC in the Denver area can help you troubleshoot and coordinate everything, even handling countertop cutouts or plumbing adjustments to make sure it all fits perfectly.",
